lesson physics-the-angle-a-mirror-sends-light v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=5 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The angle a mirror sends light back at"
  goal "Predict where a beam goes after a flat mirror, using the rule that it leaves at the same angle to the upright line as it came in."

  look {
    scene 34x12 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        mirrorangle m angle=40 targets=[20, 40, 60] marks=1 show=1
        observatory o open=1
      }
    }
    say """
      Light travels in straight lines until it meets something. When a beam meets a flat mirror,
      picture a line standing straight up from the mirror where the beam lands. The beam leaves on
      the other side of that upright line, at exactly the same angle as it came in: in at 40°, out at
      40°. That one rule tells you where every beam goes, and it is how the observatory's
      periscope and mirrors send light where it is wanted.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked physics.reflect-out a=30 level=medium
    }
    practice physics.reflect-out count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3 easy-count=2
    practice physics.reflect-target count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.reflect-between count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.reflect-from-mirror count=2 seed=1 easy-count=1
    practice physics.periscope-turn count=1 seed=1
    level hard {
      show physics.reflect-between a=35
    }
  }
  story {
    show physics.observatory-signal o=1 easy-o=0 hard-o=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.reflect-bigger a=30 u=0 easy-a=40 hard-u=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.reflect-turn-angle v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  remember {
    say "A mirror sends light out at the same angle to the upright line as it came in."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The angle that trips children up is the one they measure. The rule works from the upright line
    standing out of the mirror, and a child who measures from the mirror's surface gets the
    complement, 50° instead of 40°. One question gives the angle to the surface on purpose, so the
    child has to take it from 90° first. Listen for the rule said in full: same angle, other side of
    the upright line. If the angles are muddled, draw the upright line in pencil on every question
    and stay here another day. Off the page, a torch, a small mirror flat on a table and a sheet of
    paper to draw the beams on, with a protractor to check the two angles are equal. In the
    three-star question the turn and the gap between the beams add to 180°, and the gap is shared
    equally either side of the upright line.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example comes first, the angles are whole tens, the targets are far apart, the surface angles are tens, the signal and the two-star question start from 30° and 40°, and the three-star turn is a right angle."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the angles end in five, the targets are close together, the angles to the surface end in five, a second gap between two beams is asked, the torch is tilted to a smaller angle rather than a bigger one, and the three-star turn is 130°, which gives 25°."
  }
}
